      "id": "exam/edexcel-9ma0-01-2019-june/q2",
      "question": "<figure class=\"practice-figure\" style=\"max-width:85mm;margin:2mm auto\"><img src=\"resources/exams/edexcel-9ma0-01-2019-june/q02-figure.svg\" alt=\"Figure 1: a plot of part of the curve y = cos x on a square grid, with axes x and y and origin O; the curve has a maximum on the y-axis and crosses the x-axis on either side.\"></figure><p>Figure 1 shows a plot of part of the curve with equation <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"y=\\cos x\"></span> where <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"x\"></span> is measured in radians. Diagram 1, on the opposite page, is a copy of Figure 1.</p><ol class=\"parts\"><li>Use Diagram 1 to show why the equation<div class=\"equation\" data-math=\"\\cos x-2x-\\frac12=0\" data-display=\"true\"></div>has only one real root, giving a reason for your answer. <span class=\"marks\">(2)</span></li></ol><p>Given that the root of the equation is <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\alpha\"></span>, and that <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\alpha\"></span> is small,</p><ol class=\"parts\" start=\"2\"><li>use the small angle approximation for <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\cos x\"></span> to estimate the value of <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\alpha\"></span> to 3 decimal places. <span class=\"marks\">(3)</span></li></ol>",
