    {
      "id": "exam/edexcel-9ma0-01-2019-june/q14",
      "question": "<p>The curve <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"C\"></span>, in the standard Cartesian plane, is defined by the equation</p><div class=\"equation\" data-math=\"x=4\\sin 2y\\qquad \\frac{-\\pi}{4}&lt;y&lt;\\frac{\\pi}{4}\" data-display=\"true\"></div><p>The curve <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"C\"></span> passes through the origin <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"O\"></span></p><ol class=\"parts\"><li>Find the value of <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\dfrac{\\mathrm dy}{\\mathrm dx}\"></span> at the origin. <span class=\"marks\">(2)</span></li><li><ol class=\"parts\" type=\"i\"><li>Use the small angle approximation for <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\sin 2y\"></span> to find an equation linking <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"x\"></span> and <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"y\"></span> for points close to the origin.</li><li>Explain the relationship between the answers to (a) and (b)(i). <span class=\"marks\">(2)</span></li></ol></li><li>Show that, for all points <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"(x, y)\"></span> lying on <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"C\"></span>,<div class=\"equation\" data-math=\"\\frac{\\mathrm dy}{\\mathrm dx}=\\frac{1}{a\\sqrt{b-x^2}}\" data-display=\"true\"></div>where <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"a\"></span> and <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"b\"></span> are constants to be found. <span class=\"marks\">(3)</span></li></ol>",
